                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1][b]Fake Colours\t\t* 7/10[/b][/h1]\n\nRubik's cube evolved. That's the first thing that comes to my mind when I think of Fake Colours. Riddles based on colours and schemes, start easy and become very complicated.\n\n[h1][i]INTRO & GAMEPLAY. What is Fake Colours about And how does it work?[/i][/h1]\nFake Colour is a Puzzle game, essentially speaking, you have three cubes each of a different colour, red, blue or green. Basic rules are always explained in a clear way, however the very base is that each cube can roll on another box only if it has the same colour or if it's a white box. Cubes can roll over one of the other two cubes despite their colours. Purpose of the game is to bring the three cubes in the shiny 3 boxes on the other side of the board. Sounds easy and starts easy as well. Then it gets very difficult and ends up with a very mazy final board. However the game's not this plane. As you proceed with the boards, new features will appear, so colour switchers, lasers and elevators will start appearing, and to make things simple they all have a colour related behaviour.\n\n[h1][i]GRAPHIC. Engine And Artistic aspects.[/i][/h1]\nMade with Unity, the game is very compact and quick in loadings and performances. After all it has to move cubes on a board. It doesn't shine for using incredible effects (yes, I'm talking about lasers penetrating cubes without even a small particle effects. That's not essential but it's a pity), but it does its job.\n\n[h1][i]SOUNDTRACK. Themes and Accompaniments.[/i][/h1]\nThere's always a light atmospheric music that kind of reminds portal 2... In a very vague and distorted way. Not in a bad way, jokes aside, they do their job, don't get in the way and help setting a relaxing atmosphere. Except for the last maps where the theme sounds a bit more anguishing than the other ones.\n\n[h1][i]LAST NOTES. What doesn't work?[/i][/h1]\nNot much. The best part of simple games is that the do not aspire to something they can't be, so fake colour is exactly what you expect: 30 levels of complicated riddles to solve, following simple rules applied on small boards that will make you think \"how do I go on from here?\".\n\n[h1][i]TL;DR. Pros And Cons[/i][/h1]\n[h1]+++PROs+++[/h1][list]\n[*][b] PUZZLE.[/b] Simple to learn, difficult to solve. When you can say this of a puzzle game it means that it just works well.\n[/list]\n[h1]---CONs---[/h1][list]\n[*][b] COLD BE EVEN BETTER.[/b] If only developers applied a little more effort and fantasy with the artistic aspect, meaning a couple of visual effects more (not some useless motion blur, or godrays and lensflares, they wouldn't make any sense, but some particles or other stuff to enrich those elements that could benefit from that) and more music (possibly not some anguishing tunes) the game could be even better.\n[/list]\n\n[i]I played this game on this machine:\nLaptop Lenovo Z50\nIntel Core i5-4210U\n8Gb RAM DDR3\nGeForce 840M/Intel HD Graphics 4400\nWindows 10 Home x64[/i]\n\n[quote] This review was submitted for [url=http://steamcommunity.com/groups/gaming_until_end]Gaming Until End[/url], through the generous contribution of [url=http://steamcommunity.com/id/HellCowON]HellCow.[/url] [/quote]",
